    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d103">

                <pb facs="0209" n="114" xml:id="pg114"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.9493/103: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-18T16:00:00+08:00">April 18, 1936—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>99. 1. Press reports that Chinese Government’s <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >agrément</hi> to appointment of Kawagoe<anchor n="49" xml:id="d103-f1"/>
                    would be announced today appear premature. According to responsible official of
                    the Foreign Office, the Government will not act on the nomination before 3 or 4
                    more days. A Domei (Japanese news agency) despatch dated Nanking, April 15,
                    purports to quote an unnamed high official of the Foreign Office as saying that
                    “China is not in a position to object to Kawagoe’s appointment.”</p>
                <p>2. It seems generally to be regretted in Chinese circles that Shigemitsu was not
                    nominated Ambassador to China.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d103-f2"/> According to one
                    foreign press despatch, the Chinese believe that Kawagoe won the appointment by
                    his sympathetic attitude, as Consul General at Tientsin, toward the acts of the
                    Japanese military in North China and his appointment “is considered a further
                    surrender (presumably of the Japanese Foreign Office) of [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">to?</hi>] the Japanese military authorities.”</p>
                <p>3. Repeated to Department and Peiping, by mail to Tokyo, Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d103-f1" n="49" xml:id="d103-f1-content">
                <p>Shigeru Kawagoe’s appointment as Japanese Ambassador was confirmed May 1.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d103-f2" n="50" xml:id="d103-f2-content">
                <p>Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs, Mamoru Shigemitsu was appointed Ambassador to
                    the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
